    "evidence_text": "Glutaminolysis activates Rag-mTORC1 signaling.\n\nAmino acids control cell growth via activation of the highly conserved kinase TORC1.\n\nGlutamine is a particularly important amino acid in cell growth control and metabolism.\n\nHowever, the role of glutamine in TORC1 activation remains poorly defined.\n\nThis may provide an explanation for glutamine addiction in cancer cells.\n\nGlutamine supports pancreatic cancer growth through a Kras-regulated metabolic pathway\n\nCancer cells have metabolic dependencies that distinguish them from their normal counterparts.\n\nAmong these dependencies is an increased use of the amino acid glutamine to fuel anabolic processes.\n\nCa2+-permeable AMPA receptors regulate growth of human glioblastoma via Akt activation.\n\nThis novel pathway might give an alternative therapeutic target.\n\nTaken together, this demonstrates that PKC\u03b6 is a critical metabolic tumor suppressor in mouse and human cancer.\n\nProteomic analysis reveals Warburg effect and anomalous metabolism of glutamine in pancreatic cancer cells.\n\nMutations in cancer reprogram amino acid metabolism to drive tumor growth, but the molecular mechanisms are not well understood.\n\nTargeted inhibition of tumor-specific glutaminase diminishes cell-autonomous tumorigenesis.\n\nTargeting metabolic remodeling in glioblastoma multiforme.",
